Yeah: don't fix them until after you've enchanted them.

If they're +3, you have 2 options:
Fix them.  They'll give you 4 AC now and 4 AC later after you run
through some fire traps.
Enchant them: They'll give you at least 5 AC now, and 4 AC later after
you run through some fire traps.  And if you get 2 points from the ?ea,
they'll give 6AC now and 5AC later.

Essentially, fixing your gloves is worth less than a point of AC (or at
most a point if they're already burnt/corroded/whatever).  So it's
always preferrable to enchant them 1 or more points instead of fixing
them.  The only time to fix them is once they (and everything else in
your inventory) are maxed out on enchantment.

> I fix everything before I head into Gehennom. There are fire traps and the
> like everywhere. If they are not fireproof they'll soon be burned to a crisp.

Blessed +5 leather gloves = 6 points of AC
After you've walked through a bazillion fire traps and punched 500
puddings to death:
Blessed thoroughly burnt thoroughly corroded +5 leather gloves = 5
points of AC.

The only thing you save by fixing them is whatever the base
(unenchanted) AC value of the item is.  With robes or large shields or
other things that have a base AC of 2 or more, fixing is a somewhat
bigger deal but still not a real necessity.



